The two sources under java/ are the exception. They are compiled inside a KeY
checkout and against the KeY API, so they carry KeY's own GPL-2.0-only header,
which is also what KeY's build requires of every file in its source tree. MIT
is compatible with the GPL, so nothing here restricts what may be done with the
rest of the project; a binary produced by compiling java/ together with KeY is
a combined work and is covered by the GPL, as any such build of KeY would be.

KeY itself is not distributed here. It is cloned by the user and read by this
tool. See https://key-project.org.
